    "turn_text": "No well actually first was a small town in southern Minnesota Here's the deal. My dad, his father died when he was 16. So my dad went to work and didn't graduate high school. So he never really had a career as such. And my other grandfather and my mom's side had a quilting factory. Quilting is the fabric that uses liners for winter coats and stuff like that. So he wanted to open a factory in midwest and sent my father out. And we went to Albert Lee, Minnesota, which was in southern Minnesota, a small, very small city. And the factory failed after a couple of years. And when I was six, we moved up to St. Louis Park, which was a suburb of Minneapolis. And years later, when I finally was old enough to sort of think about this, I said, Dad, why Albert Lee? And my dad said, well, your grandfather, wanted to open a factory in the Midwest, and the railroad went through Albitley. And I said, well, why did the factory fail? And he said, it went through Albitley, but it wouldn't stop. And my dad was not a good businessman. He was not a good businessman, but he was very funny, and he was the sweetest guy in the world, lovely guy.",
